To send an Azure AD B2C token via Postman and validate the user in a C# .NET Core API, follow these steps:


---

1. Get an Azure AD B2C Token

Before you can send the token to your API, you need to obtain one. Use Postman to request an access token:

Request a Token (Authorization Code Flow)

Method: POST

URL:

https://<tenant>.b2clogin.com/<tenant>.onmicrosoft.com/<policy>/oauth2/v2.0/token

Headers:

Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

Body (x-www-form-urlencoded):

grant_type: password
client_id: <your-client-id>
client_secret: <your-client-secret>
scope: https://<tenant>.onmicrosoft.com/<api-scope>/user_impersonation offline_access
username: <your-test-user-email>
password: <your-test-user-password>
response_type: token

Click Send and copy the access_token.



---

2. Send the Token in Postman

Now, call your API with the token:

Method: GET (or POST, depending on your API)

URL:

https://your-api.com/protected-endpoint

Headers:

Authorization: Bearer <your-access-token>



---

3. Validate the Token in .NET Core API

Modify your appsettings.json:

"AzureAdB2C": {
  "Instance": "https://yourtenant.b2clogin.com",
  "ClientId": "your-client-id",
  "Domain": "yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com",
  "SignUpSignInPolicyId": "B2C_1_your_policy",
  "TenantId": "yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com",
  "Audience": "your-client-id"
}

Configure Authentication in Program.cs

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.Identity.Web;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration.GetSection("AzureAdB2C"));

builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
builder.Services.AddControllers();

var app = builder.Build();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();


---

4. Validate Token in a Controller

Create a protected API endpoint:

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Linq;

[Route("api/[controller]")]
[ApiController]
public class ProtectedController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    [Authorize]
    public IActionResult GetUserInfo()
    {
        var claims = User.Claims.Select(c => new { c.Type, c.Value });
        return Ok(claims);
    }
}


---

5. Refresh Token Implementation

If your token expires, request a new one using the refresh token:

Method: POST

URL:

https://<tenant>.b2clogin.com/<tenant>.onmicrosoft.com/<policy>/oauth2/v2.0/token

Body (x-www-form-urlencoded):

grant_type: refresh_token
client_id: <your-client-id>
client_secret: <your-client-secret>
scope: https://<tenant>.onmicrosoft.com/<api-scope>/user_impersonation offline_access
refresh_token: <your-refresh-token>


This will return a new access_token and refresh_token.


---

Final Steps

1. Get the access token in Postman.


2. Send the token to your protected API using Authorization: Bearer <token>.


3. Ensure your API is configured to validate the JWT token.


4. Use the refresh token when the access token expires.



